{"name":"Hold Your Horses","hex":"#705446","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hold your horses","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be patient; to wait.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see hold,‎ one's,‎ horses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hold_your_horses"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hold your horses","blocks":[{"text":"\"Hold your horses\", sometimes said as \"Hold the horses\", is an English-language idiom meaning \"wait, slow down\". The phrase is historically related to horse riding or travelling by horse, or driving a horse-drawn vehicle. A number of explanations, all unverified, have been offered for the origins of the phrase, dating back to usage in Ancient Greece."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hold_your_horses","wikidata":"Q5879399"},{"title":"Hold Your Horses (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Hold Your Horses is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by E. Mason Hopper and starring Tom Moore, Sylvia Ashton and Naomi Childers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Hold_Your_Horses_%281921%29_-_Ad_1.jpg/330px-Hold_Your_Horses_%281921%29_-_Ad_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hold_Your_Horses_(film)","wikidata":"Q84089628"},{"title":"You","blocks":[{"text":"In Modern English, the word \"you\" is the second-person pronoun. It is grammatically plural, and was historically used only for the dative case, but in most modern dialects is used for all cases and numbers."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You","wikidata":"Q1165897"},{"title":"Horse","blocks":[{"text":"The horse is a domesticated, one-toed, hoofed mammal. It belongs to the taxonomic family Equidae and is one of two extant subspecies of Equus ferus. The horse has evolved over the past 45 to 55 million years from a small multi-toed creature, Eohippus, into the large single-toed animal of today, originally in North America. Humans began domesticating horses around 4000 BCE in Central Asia, primarily associated with the Botai culture; their domestication is believed to have been widespread by 3000 BCE. However, contemporary genetic evidence indicates that modern domesticated breeds do not descend from this lineage, instead pointing to the Western Eurasian steppes in the lower Volga-Don region as the homeland of modern domestic horses. The modern horse breed replaced the local populations through their rapid expansion across Eurasia from 2000 BCE. Horses in the subspecies caballus are domesticated, although some domesticated populations live in the wild as feral horses. These feral populations are not true wild horses, which are horses that have never been domesticated. There is an extensive, specialized vocabulary used to describe equine-related concepts, covering everything from anatomy to life stages, size, colors, markings, breeds, locomotion, and behavior."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Nokota_Horses_cropped.jpg/330px-Nokota_Horses_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horse","wikidata":"Q726"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Points_of_a_horse.jpg/500px-Points_of_a_horse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Points_of_a_horse.jpg/500px-Points_of_a_horse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"External anatomy of a horse[3][4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoints_of_a_horse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Horse-and-pony.jpg/500px-Horse-and-pony.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Horse-and-pony.jpg/1280px-Horse-and-